  "overall": "The translation is very close to the Latin and preserves the psalter’s Septuagintal oddities rather than harmonizing them to Hebrew. The pointing marks match the Latin in the same order throughout. The only adequacy concern is the slightly narrowed rendering of “in idipsum” in 4:9.",
  "disagreementsWithTheDossier": [
    "4:2b: “Miserére” is not a proper noun but the imperative of misereri; “mei” is genitive of ego, not a possessive determiner.",
    "4:3: “Fílii hóminum” is vocative in force, not nominative subject/root; “vanitatem” is from vanitas, not vanito; “ut quid” functions idiomatically as “why / for what,” not as a simple object “what.”",
    "4:4: “me” in “Dóminus exáudiet me” is accusative object, not ablative; “cum clamávero” has temporal cum, not the preposition cum governing me.",
    "4:5: “Irascímini” is the deponent imperative 2nd plural of irasci, not a proper noun; “compungímini” is imperative in sense here, not merely indicative.",
    "4:6: “sacrificium” is the object of “Sacrificáte,” not the subject; “bona” is a neuter plural substantive, “good things.”",
    "4:10: The clause is governed by “constituísti”; “Quóniam tu, Dómine, singuláriter in spe” is not a self-standing root phrase."
